10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B1,B2}

I think Colleague 7’s A4-first line deserves more weight, and I was too quick to concede B2. The seki refutation assumes Black can simply pass after 4...B A3, but that overlooks White’s follow-up with A5 and then C5. After 1 W B2, 2 B B1, 3 W A4, if Black tries 4...B A3, White plays 5 W A5. Now Black cannot just pass forever: White threatens to connect through C5 and then reduce the upper group’s liberties. If Black fills B2, White A1 captures; if Black fills A1, White B2 captures. The two shared liberties are not true independent eyes for separate living groups because White can use the outside connection to force the order of filling. Thus B2 does initiate a kill sequence as well.

So the kill-initiating moves should be **{B1,B2}**, not only {B1}.

